<http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/sch/SCM16-6.2> a ipbes:SubChapter ;
    ipbes:Chapter <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ch/SCM16ch6> ;
    ipbes:Reference <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/2333079/items/IMJG2QCG>,
        <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/ref/2333079/items/N673JAIG> ;
    ipbes:Report <http://ontology.ipbes.net/report/> ;
    ipbes:hasDescription "Models or scenarios developed for different spatial and temporal scales, domains and for different elements (i.e. the elements of the IPBES framework, see also Figure 6.1) can be linked or harmonized using several approaches (Table 6.1). Linking works by feeding outputs of one model as input to another model, which can be done iteratively (two-way or tight coupling) or off-line (one-way or loose coupling). Models and scenarios can also be linked qualitatively (e.g., through narratives or description of storylines). Models and scenarios describing different elements may also be combined quantitatively or qualitatively to provide a more holistic assessment, as done by Integrated Assessment Models (IAMs), and more generally, Integrated Environmental Modelling approaches . Linking works by feeding outputs of one model as input to another model, which can be done iteratively (two-way or tight coupling) or off-line (one-way or loose coupling). Models and scenarios can also be linked qualitatively (e.g., through narratives or description of storylines). Integrated Environmental Modelling broadly refers to modelling approaches that represent holistic system-level thinking by using quantitative and participatory methods for defining, selecting, integrating, and processing the combination of environmental, social and economic information needed to inform decisions and policies related to the environment . Note that frequently links are being made across spatial and temporal scales, and elements may act on one another at different scales (also see IPBES/3/INF/4, http://ipbes.net/, Chapter 2). For example, historic global climate data may be used as input for modelling the current distribution of species at national scale, which then are used to estimate a provisioning ecosystem service (blue arrows in Figure 6.1). Table 6.1 Summary of different approaches to linking and harmonizing models and scenarios. Approaches Model Scenario Linking Output-input, one-way coupling (section 6.3.1) x Output-input, two-way coupling (section 6.3.1) x Combining outputs qualitatively (section 6.3.2) x x Harmonizing Standardization of metrics (input and/or output) through a) classification schemes and taxonomies; b) converting across dimensions, domains and organizational levels; c) scaling in time and space (sections 6.4.1-2) x x Benchmarking (section 6.4.3) x Harmonization of models and scenarios occurs across domains and spatial and temporal scales within an element. In particular, harmonization involves the standardization of metrics (e.g., output metrics of models, conditions for scenarios i.e. CO2 concentration, agricultural production) and input data (e.g., land use, temperature) or both. For nominal variables, this can be achieved through the adoption of standard classification schemes (e.g., the unified classification of species threats and conservation actions, . Harmonization often involves upscaling and downscaling models and scenarios in space and time, as well as model benchmarking. Benchmarking is not applicable to scenarios because these are by definition alternative to each other. Harmonized models and scenarios and their outputs facilitate model linking, error detection and uncertainty estimation and ultimately decision making. Figure 6.1: Linking models among the six elements of the IPBES conceptual framework (straight arrows) and within domains (vertical axis), spatial (horizontal) and temporal (Z dimension) scales of each element (curved arrows). Each element has multiple dimensions (examples shown) including temporal and spatial scales, and disciplinary and organizational domains. Blue arrow explained in text." ;
    dcterms:identifier "6.2" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Approaches for linking and harmonizing models and scenarios" .

