    ipbes:hasDescription "6.4.1 Harmonizing models across scales Harmonizing models to assess status and trends, and project future changes in biodiversity and ecosystem services require synthesizing biophysical and socio-economic data and results that are available at different organizational, spatial, temporal scales and domains (Figure 6.2). Scales can be defined considering two main properties: grain and extent. Grain refers to the resolution of the data and extent to the size of the dataset. More specifically, the organizational grain is the resolution of the social, human or built capital information, the spatial grain is the size of the sampling unit, the temporal grain is the frequency of data. The extent refers to how large is the human system considered (organizational extent), an area (spatial extent) or period of time (temporal extent). For example, with an Enhanced Thematic Mapper Plus sensor, the spatial grain is 30 meters (for bands 1 to 5), the temporal grain is 16 days (the satellite makes an image of the same place each 16 days), the spatial extent corresponds to a track 183 km wide, and the temporal extent is the duration of the study (for example, few days, one season, several years). Figure 6.2: Spatial, temporal and organizational scales are usually correlated, thus the consequences of changing the scale of analysis (upscaling or downscaling) in any of these three dimensions need to be carefully considered. Upscaling is related to an increase in scale extent and grain size, while downscaling is the inverse process. Note that the organizational scale, here represented by social organization (e.g., individuals, communities societies), is also relevant to biodiversity (e.g., genes, species, ecosystems) and ecosystem services (e.g., provisioning, regulating, cultural). Space, time and organizational scales are usually correlated (Figure 6.2; The assessment of large human systems or communities will require data at large spatial and temporal scales (but with low resolution), and inversely data on specific local communities will require more detailed temporal and spatial information, with high resolution. As a consequence of this spatial-temporal interaction, models with coarse spatial resolution usually do not resolve processes that operate at fine temporal scale, and vice versa. For example, global scale population dynamic models of fishes do not resolve fine scale behavioural shift of individuals driven by changing local ecological or environmental conditions. There is an optimal scale to understand specific natural dynamics of systems operating at a focal spatial scale and it is a challenge (for scientists and practitioners) to identify the appropriate scale, e.g., avoiding building too much detailed on coarse-scale models. Bringing model outputs to the appropriate scale is referred to as scaling, which can be done in two different directions: upscaling information from local, fine-grained resolution to global, coarse-grained resolution, or vice versa, downscaling the information. Upscaling usually leads to an increase in the extent, and decrease in the resolution, while downscaling increases the resolution of the data, while losing the extent (Figure 6.3). Upscaling and downscaling are discussed in detail for organizational, spatial and temporal scales in sections 6.4.1.1-6.4.1.3. In both directions, predictions are associated with errors and uncertainty, which are explored in the next section (Section 6.5). Figure 6.3: Four ecological processes (tree replacement, secondary succession, speciation and extinction) and their respective space-time domains. Large spatial and temporal extents are usually related with coarse spatial and temporal resolution data, in contrast to small spatial and temporal extents. Consequently scaling up is usually associated with an increase in spatial and temporal extent and decrease in resolution, while scaling down has as consequence a reduction in extent, which should be followed by an increase in resolution. Cross-scaling is appropriate in situation where biodiversity dynamics and ecological processes acting at a particular domain of scales are also indirectly affected by processes acting in other scales (Section 6.4.1.4). Local biodiversity pattern or ecosystem services, such as stocks and flows of water and other living resources, are mainly controlled by proximate factors acting locally, but are also affected by indirect global drivers of change , which would require data on large spatial extension and for an extended period of time. Inversely, local actions affect the environment globally, and as a consequence the success of global scenario projections will depend on the congruence of scenarios and goals planned at more local scales . 6.4.1.1 Social organizational scale The underlying assumptions and mental models that people hold about the human-natural systems relationship drives the types of models and scenarios being accepted and developed (see also IPBES Deliverable 3d on ‘diverse values and valuation’, http://ipbes.net/). Several models have been proposed and adopted to provide knowledge about human-natural systems in a range of spatial-temporal-organizational dimension . Some of these models are static, with snapshot changes (e.g., Computable General Equilibrium), linear with projected changes over time [e.g., VISIT, Integrated Valuation of Environmental Services and Trade-offs (InVEST) ] and system based models [e.g., World3; Meadows , Global Unified Meta-model of the Biosphere (GUMBO) and Multi-scale Integrated Model of Ecosystem Services (MIMES) (Box 6.3) ]. Box 6.3: Multi-scale Integrated Model for Ecosystem Services (MIMES) for Manawatu watershed, New Zealand The Manawatu River watershed is located in the North island of New Zealand and is home to about 200,000 people and the land intensively used for agriculture, particularly dairying. Historically, steep hills were forested, but forest is now down to 20% of the original cover . In 2009, researchers ranked it as the worst of 300 rivers tested for daily change in dissolved oxygen . In response, the regional government initiated a collaborative process to bring together stakeholder to improve the conditions of the river. In 2009, a newspaper article labelled the Manawatu the ‘river of shame’. Researchers had ranked it as the worst of 300 rivers tested for daily change in dissolved oxygen . In response, the regional government initiated a collaborative process to bring together stakeholder which became the Manawatu River Leadership Forum. This coincided in timing with Ministry of Business, Innovation and Employment funding Ecological Economics Research New Zealand to undertake the ‘Integrated Freshwater Solutions’ (IFS) action research project. Mediated Modelling approach was used to support the collaborative effort to understand the underlying systems driving poor water quality , specifically those causing eutrophication, erosion and habitat destruction. The Mediated Modelling scoping model was used to ‘play out’ some of the scenarios associated with the detailed ‘Action Plan’ signed off by the Manawatu River Leaders Forum. An example of policy scenario is provide funding to reduce erosion by retiring land and planting trees as part of the Sustainable Land Use Initiative. The Mediated Modelling effort with stakeholders was subsequently translated and enhanced to develop a spatially explicit, dynamic Multi-scale Integrated Model for Ecosystem Services (MIMES) . MIMES uses Simile software and links multiple databases in a way that allows the bundling and trading of ecosystem services over time and space. Here erosion control (as undertaken for example by the Sustainable Land Use Initiative programme) is mapped to highlight the change in ‘hotspots’ over time and space . The progression of model development from Mediated Modelling to MIMES required a transition from interpreting stakeholder perceptions to more data-intense, specialist, modelling by the science community. Up- and down- scaling along a social organizational scale requires awareness of humanly imposed boundaries and conventions, which often are not following an ecosystem logic and may be difficult to be clearly defined . For example, the usages of surface and ground water can have largely different spatial extent. In addition, governing bodies are often guided by multiple ways in which their constitutions are divided in space; e.g., the Auckland Council identified 30 different ways in which space is divided for water management (including water supply, water treatment, storm water, river/coastal and groundwater protection and various values from interest groups such as people from the Maori culture) . As such, optimal operational scales for model and scenarios differ in different organizational context and they should be ‘fit for purpose’. Ecosystem services approach can be considered an organizing principle for harmonization of models or their outputs along an organizational scale . An ecosystem services approach is also inherently multi-scale as ecosystem services can be classified according to their spatial characteristics ; see Figure 6.4). (1) At a global level, climate regulation, carbon sequestration and storage as well as cultural or existence values do not depend on people’s proximity to the ecosystems from where the services originate, whereas (2) local proximity is relevant for disturbance regulation / storm protection, waste treatment, pollination, biological control and habitat. (3) A directional flow characterises water regulation/ flood protection, water supply, sediment retention/erosion control or nutrient regulation. (4) a point of use is relevant for soil formation, food/forest production and other raw material and finally (5) some ecosystem services and the benefits/values derived from them are related to the manner in which users move in space (and time), e.g., genetics resources, recreational potential and cultural values. Due to its complexity, the scope for approaches aiming for optimization is limited and the process of model building with stakeholders becomes equally important as the model itself . Figure 6.4: Multiscale ecosystem services approach classified according to their spatial characteristics (adapted from . 6.4.1.2 Spatial scale Downscaling Spatial downscaling is a common technique to provide spatial information for local conservation issues or management needs, such as establishing priority conservation areas, when high resolution information is not available . For example, downscaling is relevant to incorporating projections of climate models into local conservation planning . There is a long history of developing downscaling methods for climate data that provides valuable experience for downscaling of biodiversity and ecosystem services models and scenarios (Box 6.4). Downscaling has also been applied to biodiversity assessment, such as the downscaling of Red List Index (RLI) from global to national scale . Box 6.4 Interpolation of local information from global information Introduction of global information by establishing statistical transfer functions is an efficient approach to improve the estimation error of ecological variables for locations where primary data is not available. A statistical analysis of precipitation data from the 661 meteorological stations (Figure Box 6.4a) demonstrates that precipitation has a close relationship with topographic aspect, latitude, longitude and elevation. The statistical transfer functions of mean annual precipitation under a Box-Cox transformation was derived as a combination of minimized residuals output by a method for high accuracy surface modelling with the Geographically Weighted Regression using latitude, longitude, elevation, impact coefficient of aspect and sky view factor as independent variables. The introduction of spatial non-stationarity analyses into the interpolation of meteorological stations has greatly improved the interpolated climate surfaces (Figure Box 6.4c). For instance, inverse distance weighting was applied to interpolation of mean annual precipitation in China during the period from 1960 to 2010, taking a digital elevation model (DEM) as secondary data (Figure Box 6.4b); mean absolute error of the mean annual precipitation was 102.23 mm. The mean relative error of the interpolated mean annual precipitation decreased by 3% due to the combination of Geographically Weighted Regression with inverse distance weighting; in addition, when high accuracy surface modelling is used, which has much better performance comparing with the classical methods such as inverse distance weighting, Kriging and Spline, the accuracy of the interpolated mean annual precipitation has been increased by 3% . In other words, an introduction of both Geographically Weighted Regression and high accuracy surface modelling can make the accuracy of the interpolated mean annual precipitation increased by 6% totally. a) b) c) Figure Box 6.4: a) Spatial distribution of the meteorological stations with location information in China, b) Digital elevation model of China, c) Surface of mean annual precipitation in China the necessary global information have been extracted from the local information by geo-statistics. Most often downscaling techniques are based on interpolation of statistical relationships between specific model or scenario metrics and predictors with higher resolution data. For example, the expected distribution of a species inside its geographic range can be inferred from high resolution data on the distribution of its habitat; thus downscaling the information to the scale of the habitat maps (e.g., . A similar approach is obtained with hierarchical modelling which projects the relationship between coarse-grain species and environmental data to a finer grain using fine-grain environmental (predictor) variables. This method was used for downscaling exploited fishes and invertebrates’ distributions in Western Australia . Similarly, Barwell downscaled a coarse-grained (> 100 km2) Odonata atlas data to a more fine-grain (25km2, 4km2 and 1 km2) local scale in mainland Britain, suggesting reasonable estimates of fine-grain occupancy, with varying errors according to species traits. Recent studies showed high predictive performance of downscaling models in comparison with field observations of invasive alien species , birds , Sardinian reptiles , and global marine circulation . Specific methods such as Hierarchical Bayesian Modelling (HBM) approach is shown to improve the performance of downscaling over other statistical methods . Those predictions may be further improved when combined with macro-ecological relationships (e.g., scale area relationships) . Dynamic downscaling that is based on mechanistic models may be more appropriate than statistical downscaling in systems where the relationship between coarse scale and fine scale dynamics are complex and non-linear or observational data are insufficient. Dynamics downscaling uses fine-resolution dynamical models to estimate fine-scale dynamical features . Available methods of downscaling involve developing fine-scale models that are forced with coarse global climate simulations, or forcing a fine-resolution model component with information from coarse resolution model. The coupling between coarse and fine scale models can be ‘one-way’ or ‘two-way’. Dynamic downscaling has been applied widely in regional climate and oceanographic modelling. On one hand, dynamic downscaling offers consistency of the solutions and reliance upon fundamental principles of physics, chemistry, biology and ecology. On the other hand, it requires higher computational cost of running the models. Also, the coupled coarse-fine scale models are more complex and costly to develop. Furthermore, while dynamical downscaling may improve the representation of fine-scale dynamics, it is still strongly influenced by any biases in the coarse-scale simulations used for the boundary forcing. While downscaling methodology is well developed, there is a trade-off between the cost to collect data and develop models at fine scale on one hand, and the uncertainty of downscaled outputs on the other. The decision on which scale to adopt ultimately depends on the resources available and the acceptable level of error, which can be quantified by validation of the downscaled model with sampled high resolution data. Upscaling Environmental problems or the consequences of human activities sometimes encompass broad spatial and temporal scales, which need global assessments and policy actions. For this reason, it is often necessary to transfer local high-resolution data to broader scales, which is called spatial upscaling . Upscaling methods are more intuitive than downscaling ones, as they involve extrapolating values over a larger space. Upscaling methods can be categorized into four main types: coarse graining, lagging, accumulating and rating (Figure 6.5). Coarse graining increases the spatial extent of the unit. Lagging increases the separation between units. Accumulating sums all the finer scale values within a larger spatial extent. Rating compares data on distinct units that differ in size of some other characteristic to develop scaling functions . Figure 6.5: Comparison of four main methods of upscaling (redrawn from Schneider ). Spatial upscaling approaches have been commonly applied to analyse satellite imagery and combine statistical and image processing analyses with simulation models and field observations . For example, upscaling has been used to estimate net ecosystem exchange or carbon dioxide fluxes from flux towers to landscape and regional scales . In another example, a simple exponential relationship between Leaf Area Index (LAI) and the Normalized Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI) obtained with a Landsat image was used to upscale LAI values to Arctic landscapes . Other methods have been developed to upscale gross ecosystem production (GEP) from leaf or stand levels to larger regions (ca. 12 km2) taking into account tree canopy structure , using Light Detection and Ranging (LiDAR) images. Results showed a high correlation (r2 between 0.75 and 0.91, p< 0.05) between estimated and measured ecosystem production. A good fit between upscaled estimated values and field measurements was also obtained with net primary productivity in China, showing that the integration of field data with remote sensing though an ecosystem model can generate reliable estimates . Upscaling can result in better estimates than those obtained from coarse-grained resolution images, such as those obtained from satellite remote sensing data from the Moderate Resolution Imaging Spectroradiometer (MODIS) , possibly because they can integrate the variability observed at finer scales in the coarse-scale evaluation. Similar results were obtained by Hay when upscaling forest stand characteristics with image resampling techniques. They showed that appropriately upscaled satellite imagery can represent a more accurate estimation than an image obtained at the upscaled resolution. However, it is costly to have fine scale information for large extent for upscaling to reveal broad scale pattern. In the process of upscaling, quantitative approaches to preserve the quality of the original information should be applied whenever possible; otherwise it can contribute to scaling uncertainties (Section 6.5). For example, using an approach called modelled net ecosystem exchange, properties of mean, variance and skewness of fine-scale normalized difference vegetation index in an Arctic tundra landscape is preserved . 6.4.1.3 Temporal scale Quantifying and forecasting temporal change in biodiversity and ecosystem services through linking and harmonizing models and scenarios at an appropriate scale are important not only to address ecological issues, but also to develop policies and achieve global conservation goals. Depending on the properties of direct and indirect drivers (Chapters 3 and 4), the mechanisms through which these drivers result in changes in biodiversity ad ecosystem services, and the policy context, the appropriate temporal scales for models and scenarios vary (from daily, monthly, annual, decadal and centennial). Upscaling Upscaling methods across temporal scales are in principle similar to those for spatial scale which include accumulating, coarse-graining, lagging and rating (Figure 6.5). Accumulating involves summing data or model outputs from finer temporal intervals to present longer-term average conditions. For example, species occurrence records are accumulated over a long time-period (e.g., multiple decades) before being used to predict current distribution range. Coarse-graining involves averaging estimates overall smaller temporal units e.g., averaging annual climate data to calculate climatology. For lagging, multiple snap-shots are used to present changes over a longer time period. In some cases, these snap-shots could be generated from a diversity of sources, ranging from formal quantitative measurements, model outputs, or expert knowledge. For rating, quantitative functions are developed o rescale finer temporal resolution data to estimates over longer time period. For example, time trends of point information on soil solution data were scaled by linking them to soil chemical data which was available in higher temporal resolution, using both statistical and process-oriented methods . When fine-scale data or outputs are available, the cost of temporal upscaling is relatively low; however, the temporal characteristics of the data across scales should be considered carefully. Otherwise, upscaling may contribute to scaling error (see 6.5). Specifically, the temporal variance of the data may be smoothed after upscaling. For example, seasonal differences in net primary production will not be represented in annual averages. On the other hand, higher internal variability may also need larger temporal samples for upscaling. Downscaling Downscaling temporal data is primarily based on numerical (mechanistic) models, statistical analysis and stochastic algorithms. For instance, Rebora , developed a new spatial-temporal downscaling procedure, called RainFARM to flood forecasting, as alternative to stochastic algorithms. RainFARM generates small scale rain rate fluctuations that preservers the spatio-temporal evolution of rainfall patterns. Mendes and Marengo proposed an alternative to numerical models, and developed a temporal neural network for downscaling global climate outputs (downscaling daily precipitation time series). A novel conceptual and analytical model of biodiversity loss based on the landscape ontogeny, Terragen, is currently being developed by Rosa and aims to generate biodiversity scenarios for the humid tropics, partially based on downscaling temporal data on biodiversity loss and deforestation models. However, most existing examples of temporal downscaling are related to modelling of drivers such as climate, while examples of biodiversity and ecosystem services models and scenarios are limited. 6.4.1.4 Cross-scale interactions When system processes (biophysical and/or social-economic) interact across scales (spatial, temporal or organizational), resulting in nonlinear dynamics, harmonizing of models and their outputs across these scales is more prompt to scaling error , see section 6.5.2). In such cases, the use of multiple scale models performs better than single scale models. For example, Boscolo and Metzger showed that multiscale models that considered pattern-process relationships at different extents in a unique model always performed better than single-scale model to predict the occurrences of bird species in a tropical forest. This is likely because extinction and recolonization processes that control species occurrences simultaneously act at different scales. There are many other examples of important ecological processes that are modulated by processes that interact across scales, such as bark beetle eruptions , parasitism , fire disturbances , runoff and erosion processes (see Box 6.5). Box 6.5: Regional assessments that includes both global information and local information Ecosystem services are controlled by a combination of global and local factors. The system dynamics that generate the ecosystem services cannot be recovered from the global or local controls alone . In this box, we illustrate how harmonizing models across multiple scales can improve the accuracy of the model outputs. Results from satellite-observation-based approach (global scale) and a local-information-based approach (local scale) (Fang, 2001) were combined means of high accuracy surface modelling . China’s national forest inventory database from 2004 to 2008 includes 160,000 permanent sample plots and 90,000 temporary sample plots scattered over the land surface of China. The cross-validation was comprised of four steps: (i) 5% of the sample plots of each forest type in each province were removed for validation prior to model creation; (ii) the spatial distribution of average forest carbon stocks (CS) in China during the period 2004-2008 was simulated at a spatial resolution of 5km×5km using the remaining 95% of the sample plots; (iii) the mean absolute error and mean relative error were calculated using the 5% validation set; and (iv) the 5% validation set was returned to the pool of available sample plots for the next iteration, and another5 % validation set was removed. This process was repeated until all of the sample plots were used for validation at least one time and the simulation error statistics for each sample plot could be calculated. The mean absolute errors of the carbon stock surfaces, generated by the satellite-observation-based approach global-information-based method (Figure Box 6.5a) and the Kriging local-information-based method (Figure Box 6.5b), are respectively 1.9 and 2.0 kg·m-2. When the local information is combined into satellite-observation-based approach by means of high accuracy surface modelling, which is denoted below as high accuracy surface modelling - satellite-observation-based approach (Figure Box 6.5c), the mean absolute error is decreased to 0.9 kg·m-2. The mean relative errors of both the global and local- information-based methods have been reduced by at least 53% because the local and global information were fused by means of high accuracy surface modelling. In terms of high accuracy surface modelling - satellite-observation-based approach, annual mean carbon stocks of all forest types in China was 7.1 Pg during the period 2004-2008, given contributions of 2.7, 4.0, and 0.4 Pg from coniferous, broadleaf and mixed forests, respectively. Similarly, the annual mean carbon density was 4.6 kg/m2 during the period 2004-2008, with contributions of 4.4, 4.7, and 4.2 kg/m2 from coniferous, broadleaf, and mixed forests respectively. The satellite-observation-based approach underestimates annual mean carbon stocks, whereas Kriging overestimates the annual mean carbon stocks of China. a)b)c) Figure Box 6.5: Surfaces of carbon stocks created by different methods: a) satellite-observation-based approach, b) Kriging and c) high accuracy surface modelling - satellite-observation-based approach Multi-scale models can also be used to represent interactions between human organizational scales in assessment of ecosystem services. The management of salmon resource in the Columbia River Basin, USA, is a good example . Conflict within and among groups of individuals and organizations that have different interests, values, and power can be viewed as an interacting hierarchical structure. Particularly, the interests of local loggers, fishers and environmentalists are in conflict with the interests of those who are planning hydropower utilities, as well as pitting native fishers against offshore fishermen and environmental groups , resulting in non-linear dynamics of the social-ecological system . The use of multi-scale modelling approaches can help to consider the different factors and their interactions that are important at different scales. A drawback in using multi-scale model is that cross-scale interacting processes are difficult to model accurately and may result in error propagation as model complexity increases (see section 6.5.2). 6.4.2 Harmonization of scenarios To compare, synthesize or combine existing assessments of biodiversity and ecosystem services that use scenarios with different objectives, policy questions, assumptions, uncertainties, or focus on different temporal and spatial scales, the scenarios need to be harmonized first. Scenarios that are related to biodiversity and ecosystem services have been produced and used in different international (e.g., Millennium Ecosystem Assessment), national (e.g., the United Kingdom’s Alternative Future Scenarios for Marine Ecosystems, Pinnegar and local assessments (e.g., Manawatu basin management, Box 6.3). The differences in objectives e.g., assessing greenhouse gas emission or fisheries sustainability, assumptions, and use of different scenario development methodology may render direct comparison between these scenarios difficult. Many initiatives employed different methodologies in developing scenarios, even between different iterations of the assessment, depending primarily on the goals, spatial scales, social-economic and policy context, and resources available for the scenario development exercises (see Chapter 3). On the other hand, these scenarios may need to be combined for comprehensive assessments that include different elements and domains relating to biodiversity, ecosystem services and human well-being (Figure 6.1). Harmonization of these scenarios thus becomes important. Existing scenarios belonging to the same archetype or family can be harmonized to provide more comprehensive descriptions of possible futures . Available literature on standardizing and harmonizing scenarios for environmental assessments suggested three main steps: (1) identify and discuss the application of the scenarios and their main characteristics (2) compare the key assumptions and storylines behind the scenarios, and (3) compare the trends observed in the main scenario methodology in relation to policy making . Scenarios can be categorized into ‘scenario families’ or archetypes according to their underlying assumptions, storyline, logic, and characteristics. Some of the key assumptions and variables in which these scenarios differ include risk-perception and resulting policy actions to environmental change, spatial scale of drivers and systems, their trends, and degree of cooperation in the society . Scenarios describing plausible futures for different spatial scales can be harmonized, although existing literature largely discusses methods for downscaling. To downscale scenarios , scenario pathways at large scale can be used as boundary conditions to frame developments of finer-scale scenarios. This ensures that outcomes of the regional scenarios do not conflict with those of the global scenarios. Also, in some cases, with the help of experts and/or stakeholders participation, large scale scenario pathway can be contextualized and applied to specific region or issues. For example, through an interdisciplinary expert workshop, different Shared Socio-economic Pathways (SSPs) developed for the IPCC were converted into different scenario pathways for ocean and fisheries. Moreover, scenarios at different scale may be developed without much reference to one another but they can then be mapped together (see section 6.4). In other cases, large scale scenarios can be applied directly to examine regional policies without the need for development of complete regional scenarios e.g., application of Representative Concentration Pathways (RCPs) to assess climate change impacts at both global and regional scales. Mapping of scenarios onto archetypes or families could be facilitated using tabular or graphical representation. For example, existing scenarios for global environmental assessments include the Global Scenario Group (GSG)’s work on Great Transitions , the IPCC Special Report on Emissions Scenarios (SRES) , UNEP’s Third Global Environmental Outlook (GEO3) and the World Water Vision work (Table 6.3). To harmonize these scenarios, firstly, they are characterized by eight broad attributes: economic development, population growth, technological development, main objectives, environmental protection, trade, policies and institutions, and vulnerability to climate change (rows in Table 6.3). Based on these attributes, they can be categorized into different archetypes or scenario families: Global Sustainable Development, Business As Usual, Regional Competition, Economic Optimism, Reformed Markets and Regional Sustainability (different columns in Table 6.3). The IPCC has developed multiple sets of socio-economic scenarios for different assessment reports e.g., Special Report on Emission Scenarios (SRES, developed in Fourth Assessment Report) and Shared Socio-economic Pathways (SSPs, developed in the Fifth Assessment Report) . These IPCC scenarios can be characterized and mapped graphically according to the underlying socio-economic challenges for mitigation and adaption of each scenario (Figure 6.6). Figure 6.6.An example illustrating the mapping of scenarios onto scenario families or archetype based on the storyline, assumption and logic of the scenarios. The example is on mapping the Special Report on Emissions Scenarios (SRES) and Shared Socio-economic Pathways (SSPs) developed by the IPCC (based on . Table 6.3 Archetypes or families of scenarios from previous global environmental assessments and their key characteristics and assumptions (adopted from . Archetype/scenario family Global sustainable development Business as usual Regional competition Economic optimism Reformed markets Regional sustainability Economic development Ranging from slow to rapid Medium Slow Very Rapid Rapid Medium Population growth Medium High Low Low Low Medium Technological development Ranging from medium to rapid Medium Slow Rapid Rapid Ranging from slow to rapid Main objectives Global sustainability Not defined Security Economic growth Various goals Local sustainability Environmental protection Proactive Both reactive and proactive Reactive Reactive Both reactive and proactive Proactive Trade Globalization Weak globalization Trade barriers Globalization Globalization Trade barriers Policies and institutions Strong global governance Mixed Strong national governments Policies create open markets Policies target market failures Local action Vulnerability to climate change Low Medium Mixed – varies regionally Local action Low Low Examples SSPs SSP1 SSP2 SSP3/SSP4 SSP5 SRES B1 (A1T) B2 A2 A1F1 B2 GEO3/GEO4 Sustainability First Security First Market First Policy First Global Scenario Group New Sustainability Paradigm Barbarization Conventional World Policy Reform Eco-communalism Millennium assessment Techno-garden Order from Strength Global Orchestration Adapting Mosaic Some of the methods for downscaling scenarios can also be applied to upscale scenarios from finer to broader spatial scales, however, existing examples of scaling of scenario have greater emphasis on downscaling than on upscaling, limiting the available experience that could be drawn on. Generally, to upscale finer scale scenarios to large spatial scale, teams of developers can collaboratively develop finer scale scenarios that are consistent across regions. These local and regional scale scenarios then collectively provide description of future at global level. This method of upscaling can minimize conflict of local scale context with larger scale assumptions, while diversity of local scale context can be represented. However, substantial resources and effort are needed to coordinate the development and aggregation of multiple local scale scenarios. In the case of multi-scale scenarios, different scenarios components are kept at their most appropriate scale (space and time) with linkages between scales being established upfront . 6.4.3 Benchmarking of models Benchmarking is the process of systematically comparing sets of model predictions against measured data in order to evaluate model performance. It should also help to identify processes that may be poorly represented in models . Benchmarking is common practice in fields other than ecology: for example, Global Circulation Models included in the IPCC need to be able to hind-cast historic temperature trends, derived from multiple sources. In scenario work, model predictions are sometimes weighted by the model performance in relation to the benchmarks (e.g., . General guidelines for benchmarking environmental models have been developed by Bennett and a particular framework for land (ecosystem) models by Luo but we are not aware of any multi-model benchmarking activity with biodiversity models at the species level or ecosystem service models. The framework proposed by Luo and colleagues as part of the International Land Model Benchmarking (ILAMB) project includes 1) targeted aspects of model performance to be evaluated, (2) a set of benchmarks as defined references to test model performance, (3) metrics to measure and compare performance skills among models, and (4) model improvement. To improve the credibility of species-based biodiversity and ecosystem service models, benchmarking should be further developed. Species distribution models, for example, could be tested against observed historical changes in species ranges . This also highlight the needs for good empirical data such as from remote sensing (e.g., satellite products and aerial photos). Benchmarking should be accompanied by standardized model documentation and archiving of model source codes, input data, model results and model result processing tools. For biogeochemical models, such as global terrestrial carbon cycle models, guidelines for developing standardized archives were suggested by Thornton (2005). For biodiversity and ecosystem service models, the current situation is unsatisfactory. Even though the results from numerical models should in principle be 100% reproducible, this is often not the case, e.g., because complex models are often under constant development, implying that references to published model descriptions are outdated. Archives for this purpose still have to be developed ." ;
    dcterms:identifier "6.4" ;
    skos:prefLabel "Harmonizing models and scenarios" .

